	    MidnightBSD maintainer's guide to OpenSSH-portable
	    ==============================================

    Use separate worktrees for vendor/openssh and master.  Keep unrelated
    checkout changes out of the import.  Read the repository contribution
    policy and obtain approval before commits, merges, tags and pushes.

01) Download the OpenSSH-portable release tarball and detached signature:

    https://ftp.openbsd.org/pub/OpenBSD/OpenSSH/portable/openssh-10.6p2.tar.gz
    https://ftp.openbsd.org/pub/OpenBSD/OpenSSH/portable/openssh-10.6p2.tar.gz.asc

    Verify the release signing key against the upstream release announcement
    before trusting it, then verify both the signature and tarball:

    $ gpg --verify openssh-10.6p2.tar.gz.asc openssh-10.6p2.tar.gz

02) Unpack the verified archive outside the source worktrees.  Import the
    complete tarball into vendor/openssh, preserving the worktree's .git
    file.  Review added and deleted files before staging.  The vendor branch
    contains unmodified upstream sources, including generated configure,
    config.h.in, rendered *.0 manual pages and upstream .github files.

03) Commit the vendor import and create the annotated release tag:

    vendor/openssh/10.6p2

    Include the required DCO sign-off and AI assistance trailer when
    applicable.  Push only after approval.

04) Merge vendor/openssh into the isolated master worktree, using the
    crypto/openssh subtree.  Preserve MidnightBSD's native build files and
    patches listed below.  The master copy intentionally omits .github/,
    generated configure, config.h.in and rendered *.0 manual pages; keep
    these out of the master import while retaining them on the vendor branch.

05) Resolve conflicts and compare the result with the vendor tag.  Review
    every retained local difference, including version.h, configuration
    templates, manual pages, login policy, TCP wrappers and blocklist support.
    Bump SSH_VERSION_MIDNIGHTBSD and the documented VersionAddendum defaults.

06) Regenerate the native configuration from the merged sources:

    $ sh midnightbsd-configure.sh

    This recreates configure and config.h.in temporarily with autotools,
    generates config.h, and separates Kerberos and security-key definitions
    into krb5_config.h and sk_config.h.  Review the generated differences;
    do not add the temporary configure files or configure/build artifacts
    to master.  Do not define LIBWRAP in config.h: secure/ssh.mk selects
    TCP wrappers through MK_TCP_WRAPPERS.

07) Compare the vendor Makefile.in source lists with secure/ Makefiles.
    Update native library and program source lists for additions, removals
    and code moved between them.  See the 10.6p1 notes below.

08) Regenerate the libssh symbol namespace:

    $ sh midnightbsd-namespace.sh

    The script builds libssh without namespace mappings, extracts symbols,
    then rebuilds and checks that every library text symbol is prefixed.
    Use an isolated object directory when validating a temporary worktree.
    Link all consumer checks to that new libssh rather than the installed
    archive.  Pay particular attention to pam_ssh(8), which uses libssh
    internals and depends on complete ssh_namespace.h mappings.

09) Build the native library, programs and helpers.  Check client/server
    configuration parsing and defaults, key generation, agent operations
    and an isolated client/server session.  Include GSSAPI coverage when
    validating GSSAPI authentication fixes.  Full-world and runtime testing
    should be recorded separately from component builds.

0A) Check release/ and other consumers for obsolete options, update
    MIDNIGHTBSD-upgrade, and add a separate UPDATING entry for security or
    user-visible upgrade requirements.  Review the final diff and validation
    results before the master merge is committed and published.



	  An overview of MidnightBSD changes to OpenSSH-portable
	  ==================================================

0) VersionAddendum

   The SSH protocol allows for a human-readable version string of up
   to 40 characters to be appended to the protocol version string.
   MidnightBSD takes advantage of this to include a date indicating the
   "patch level", so people can easily determine whether their system
   is vulnerable when an OpenSSH advisory goes out.  Some people,
   however, dislike advertising their patch level in the protocol
   handshake, so we've added a VersionAddendum configuration variable
   to allow them to change or disable it.  Upstream added support for
   VersionAddendum on the server side, but we also support it on the
   client side.

1) Modified server-side defaults

   We've modified some configuration defaults in sshd:

      - UsePAM defaults to "yes".
      - PermitRootLogin defaults to "no".
      - X11Forwarding defaults to "no".
      - PasswordAuthentication defaults to "no".
      - VersionAddendum defaults to "MidnightBSD-YYYYMMDD".
      - Privilege separation and sandboxing follow the mandatory upstream
        implementation; the old UsePrivilegeSeparation option is obsolete.
      - UseDNS defaults to "yes".

2) Modified client-side defaults

   We've modified some configuration defaults in ssh:

      - CheckHostIP defaults to "no".
      - VerifyHostKeyDNS defaults to "no"; LDNS support remains available
        when built with MK_LDNS.
      - VersionAddendum defaults to "MidnightBSD-YYYYMMDD".

3) Canonic host names

   We've added code to ssh.c to canonicize the target host name after
   reading options but before trying to connect.  This eliminates the
   usual problem with duplicate known_hosts entries.

4) setusercontext() environment

   Our setusercontext(3) can set environment variables, which we must
   take care to transfer to the child's environment.

5) TCP wrappers

   Support for TCP wrappers was removed in upstream 6.7p1.  We've
   added it back by porting the 6.6p1 code forward.

   TCP wrappers support in sshd will be disabled in HEAD and will
   be removed from MidnightBSD in the future.

6) Agent client reference counting

   We've added code to ssh-agent.c to implement client reference
   counting; the agent will automatically exit when the last client
   disconnects.

7) Class-based login restrictions (27ceebbc2402)

   We've added code to auth.c to enforce the host.allow, host.deny,
   times.allow and times.deny login class capabilities, based on an
   upstream submission from
   https://github.com/openssh/openssh-portable/pull/262.

8) HPN

   We no longer have the HPN patches (adaptive buffer size for
   increased throughput on high-BxD links), but we recognize and
   ignore HPN-related configuration options to avoid breaking existing
   configurations.

9) AES-CBC

   The AES-CBC ciphers were removed from the server-side proposal list
   in 6.7p1 due to theoretical weaknesses and the availability of
   superior ciphers (including AES-CTR and AES-GCM).  We have re-added
   them for compatibility with third-party clients.

10) Native build compatibility

   MidnightBSD has no <util.h>.  Preserve HAVE_UTIL_H guards around these
   includes; fmt_scaled() is supplied by openbsd-compat.  Keep "hostfile.h"
   before "auth.h" wherever auth.h uses HostStatus.  Do not alphabetize
   upstream include blocks.

   Upstream now supplies ed25519.c and misc-agent.c.  Use the upstream
   implementations instead of restoring old local versions.  hash.c has
   been absent from upstream's library list since 10.3p1; do not restore it.
   Keep the native openbsd-compat header naming consistent with the imported
   source files.

11) OpenSSH 10.6p1 integration

   a) Server options now use SSHD_CONFIG_ENTRIES macro tables in servconf.h
      for structure declarations, defaults, parsing and serialization.
      Preserve local defaults there: UsePAM=yes, PermitRootLogin=no,
      PasswordAuthentication=no and UseDNS=yes.  Keep their configuration
      comments and manual pages consistent.  Adding local fields only to
      old manual fill/copy code is insufficient.

   b) Register UseBlocklist in the option table, with its existing global
      scope and default of no, and retain UseBlacklist as its compatibility
      alias.  Keep the blocklist_client.h calls and conditional native
      blocklist linkage in the authentication/session programs.

   c) Remove deleted ssh-dss.c from secure/lib/libssh/Makefile.  Add
      libcrux-mlkem-mldsa.c, ssh-mldsa-eddsa.c, kexmlkem768ecdh.c and
      ssherr-libcrypto.c.  Move misc-agent.c into libssh and remove its
      duplicate executable source entries.  Import the upstream libcrux
      headers and review the generated ML-KEM/ML-DSA configuration defines.

   d) Regenerate ssh_namespace.h after these source-list and configuration
      changes so that the new cryptographic, agent and error helpers are
      namespaced as well.

12) OpenSSH 10.6p2 integration

   Security and bug-fix release; configure.ac and Makefile.in are
   unchanged from 10.6p1, so config.h, krb5_config.h, sk_config.h,
   the secure/ source lists and ssh_namespace.h were not regenerated.
   shadow_pw() now returns an allocated copy that callers must free.
   ssh-mldsa44-ed25519 is added to the default CA signature
   algorithms; it depends on USE_MLDSA (defines.h), not on OpenSSL.

